workedExamples.solution_text:
The interval contains three complete $360^{\circ}$ cycles. Both endpoints have sine value $0$, so neither equation gains or loses a solution at an endpoint.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{guidered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{guidegreen}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\begin{axis}[width=11cm,height=5.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black},xlabel={$\theta\ (^{\circ})$},ylabel={$y$},xmin=-375,xmax=745,ymin=-1.3,ymax=1.3,xtick={-360,-180,0,180,360,540,720},ytick={-1,0.6,1},grid=major,grid style={gridgrey},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black,fill=white},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},samples=721,clip=true]
\addplot[curveblue,thick,domain=-360:720] {sin(x)};
\draw[guidered,dashed] (axis cs:-360,0.6)--(axis cs:720,0.6);
\draw[guidegreen,dash dot] (axis cs:-360,1)--(axis cs:720,1);
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,black] coordinates {(-323.130102,0.6) (-216.869898,0.6) (36.869898,0.6) (143.130102,0.6) (396.869898,0.6) (503.130102,0.6)};
\addplot[only marks,mark=square*,black] coordinates {(-270,1) (90,1) (450,1)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) $y=0.6$ meets the curve twice per cycle. There are $2\times3=6$ solutions.

(b) $y=1$ meets the curve once per cycle, at each maximum. There are $1\times3=3$ solutions.

